Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. SCROOGE'S COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

A period live-action London counting-house. SCROOGE, an elderly, thin, sharp-faced man with white wiry hair, blue lips, and a black coat, works beside a tiny fire. BOB CRATCHIT, slight and threadbare in a white comforter, copies letters in an unheated cell. Above the door: SCROOGE AND MARLEY.

(SOUND: wind presses against the windows; a church clock strikes three)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Marley was dead: to begin with. Old Marley was as dead as a door-nail.

A ruddy young man, FRED, enters through the fog, smiling beneath his warm brown coat.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure.

SCROOGE
What right have you to be merry? What reason have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
What right have you to be dismal? What reason have you to be morose? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
What's Christmas time to you but a time for paying bills without money; a time for finding yourself a year older, but not an hour richer?

FRED
Christmas time is a good time; a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time. And therefore, uncle, though it has never put a scrap of gold or silver in my pocket, I believe that it has done me good, and will do me good; and I say, God bless it!

Scrooge turns away. Fred keeps his good humor and exits.

(SOUND: the counting-house door shuts; a cheerful greeting fades into the fog)

Two portly gentlemen enter with books and papers.

CHARITY GENTLEMAN
At this festive season, it is more than usually desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Are there no prisons?

CHARITY GENTLEMAN
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? Are they still in operation?

CHARITY GENTLEMAN
They are. Still, I wish I could say they were not.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

CHARITY GENTLEMAN
Many can't go there; and many would rather die.

SCROOGE
It's not my business. Good afternoon, gentlemen!

The gentlemen withdraw. Scrooge resumes his ledgers as the fog thickens around the windows.

(SOUND: pen scratches over paper; distant bells and muffled footsteps)

EXT. SCROOGE'S HOUSE - NIGHT

SCROOGE, still in his black coat, gropes through the narrow, frozen yard toward the black gateway of his lonely house.

(SOUND: sleet taps the iron gate; a key scrapes in the lock)

The large brass knocker ripples before his eyes. It is no longer a knocker but the livid face of JACOB MARLEY, with ghostly spectacles and staring eyes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Scrooge had seen it night and morning during his whole residence there. Yet now, without intermediate change, it was Marley's face.

Scrooge blinks. The knocker is ordinary again. He unlocks the door and disappears inside.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge, now in a dressing-gown, slippers, and nightcap, checks the gloomy room: under the table, beneath the sofa, inside the closet. Nothing. He locks and double-locks the door, then sits beside a dying fire with a bowl of gruel.

(SOUND: the low fire clicks; the house settles in the darkness)

A disused bell begins to swing. Every bell in the house answers.

(SOUND: bells ring together, then cease; a heavy chain drags upward from the cellar)

The cellar door booms open. The chain climbs the stairs and passes through Scrooge's locked door. MARLEY appears: transparent, wrapped at the jaw, pigtail and waistcoat bristling, a chain of cash-boxes, keys, ledgers, deeds, and heavy purses wound around him.

(SOUND: steel links clank against the floor; the flame leaps and falls)

SCROOGE
What do you want with me?

MARLEY
Much!

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY
Ask me who I was.

SCROOGE
Who were you then?

MARLEY
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
You're an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese. There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are!

MARLEY
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link and yard by yard; I girded it on of my own free will, and of my own free will I wore it.

SCROOGE
Why do spirits walk the earth?

MARLEY
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellowmen. If that spirit goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death.

Marley lifts the chain. Scrooge stares through his transparent waistcoat at the fire behind him.

MARLEY
Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence were, all, my business.

SCROOGE
But you were always a good man of business, Jacob.

MARLEY
Business! You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

SCROOGE
Couldn't I take 'em all at once, and have it over, Jacob?

MARLEY
Expect the first to-morrow, when the bell tolls One. The second on the next night at the same hour. The third upon the next night when the last stroke of Twelve has ceased to vibrate.

Marley glides backward toward the window. It opens by itself. Beyond it, chains and phantoms drift through the fog.

(SOUND: distant lamentations rise beneath the rattling chains)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Every one wore chains like Marley's Ghost. They wandered, seeking to interfere for good in human matters, and had lost the power forever.

Marley joins their mournful dirge and vanishes into the night. Scrooge slams the window and collapses onto the bed.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

SCROOGE wakes in the dark, clutching his blankets. The clock counts its quarters, then strikes One. A clear light fills the room.

(SOUND: four hollow chimes; the final stroke of One reverberates)

The bed curtains draw aside. GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST stands there: a child-sized, ancient-looking figure with a smooth young face, white hair, bare feet, a white tunic trimmed with summer flowers, a holly branch, and a bright light springing from its head.

(SOUND: a soft, uncanny hum beneath the light)

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit, sir, whose coming was foretold to me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am!

SCROOGE
Who, and what are you?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Your purpose?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your reclamation, then. Take heed!

The Spirit clasps Scrooge's arm.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Rise! And walk with me!

Scrooge reaches for the wall. The Spirit touches his heart. The wall dissolves into white winter light.

INT. ABANDONED SCHOOLROOM - DAY

A bare schoolroom lies under snowlight. YOUNG SCROOGE, a small, thin boy with dark hair and a worn brown jacket, sits alone beside a feeble fire, reading.

(SOUND: a half-thawed water-spout drips; mice rustle behind the paneling)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A solitary child, neglected by his friends, is left there still.

SCROOGE
I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

The Spirit points. A little girl, FAN, bright-eyed and bundled for travel, bursts through the door and embraces the boy.

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! Home, for good and all. Father is so much kinder than he used to be. You're never to come back here; and we're to be together all the Christmas long!

The memory melts into a warm rushing image of a country road, a coach, and the boy leaving with Fan.

SCROOGE
Poor boy!

The schoolroom returns, empty. Scrooge wipes his eyes.

SCROOGE
There was a boy singing a Christmas Carol at my door last night. I should like to have given him something. That's all.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Let us see another Christmas!

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - NIGHT

Years rush forward. YOUNG SCROOGE, now a lanky apprentice with dark hair and a brown waistcoat, stands beside the jovial FEZZIWIG, a round older man in a Welsh wig and capacious waistcoat. The warehouse transforms into a bright ballroom.

(MUSIC: a fiddle launches into a lively country dance)

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! No more work to-night. Christmas Eve, Dick. Christmas, Ebenezer! Let's have the shutters up!

Young Scrooge and the other apprentice throw open the shutters. Workers and families flood in. Fezziwig dances with his wife as the room erupts in laughter.

(SOUND: feet stamp the boards; glasses clink; the fiddler laughs into his porter)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Why! He has spent but a few pounds of your mortal money. Is that so much that he deserves this praise?

SCROOGE
Small! He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome; a pleasure or a toil. The happiness he gives is quite as great as if it cost a fortune.

The dance freezes. The Spirit's light flares.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
My time grows short. Quick!

INT. BELLE'S PARLOR - NIGHT

A later memory. SCROOGE, still young but already hard-eyed and restless, sits opposite BELLE, a delicate young woman in a mourning-colored dress.

(SOUND: a small fire burns between them; a carriage rolls away outside)

BELLE
Another idol has displaced me; and if it can cheer and comfort you in time to come, as I would have tried to do, I have no just cause to grieve.

SCROOGE
What idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one.

SCROOGE
Even if I have grown so much wiser, what then? I am not changed towards you.

BELLE
You are changed. When it was made, you were another man. I release you, with a full heart, for the love of him you once were.

Belle leaves. Young Scrooge remains alone with his ledger.

(SOUND: the parlor door closes; the fire gutters)

SCROOGE
Spirit! Show me no more!

INT. BELLE'S FAMILY PARLOR - NIGHT

The Spirit reveals BELLE, now a warm-eyed matron, surrounded by laughing children and Christmas toys. A husband returns through the door. Scrooge watches the family gather around the fire.

(MUSIC: a bright family tune, heard through laughter)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I saw an old friend of yours this afternoon. Mr. Scrooge it was. There he sat alone. Quite alone in the world, I do believe.

SCROOGE
Remove me! I cannot bear it!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I told you these were shadows of the things that have been.

Scrooge seizes the Spirit's extinguisher cap and presses it over the blazing light.

(SOUND: the light roars beneath the cap; the room falls into a deep rushing silence)

The Spirit shrinks beneath the cap. Scrooge reels backward and drops onto his own bed.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The room is transformed into a grove of holly, mistletoe, ivy, berries, puddings, roasted meats, and steaming punch. GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT, a huge, genial figure with dark curls, a green robe trimmed in white fur, a holly wreath, and a torch shaped like Plenty's horn, sits amid the feast.

(SOUND: a roaring hearth; punch bubbles; chestnuts crack)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! And know me better, man!

SCROOGE, humbled, enters the green-lit room.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. Look upon me!

SCROOGE
Have you had many brothers, Spirit?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
More than eighteen hundred.

SCROOGE
Conduct me where you will. I learnt a lesson which is working now. If you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

Scrooge grips the robe. The feast, room, fire, and night vanish.

EXT. LONDON STREET - DAY

Snow covers the city. People shovel rooftops, exchange snowballs, and carry dinners toward the bakers. The Ghost's torch sprinkles warmth over a quarrelling pair; their anger melts.

(SOUND: shovels scrape pavement; bells call the city to church)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was an air of cheerfulness abroad that the clearest summer air and brightest summer sun might have endeavoured to diffuse in vain.

SCROOGE
Is there a peculiar flavour in what you sprinkle from your torch?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
There is. My own.

SCROOGE
Would it apply to any kind of dinner on this day?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
To any kindly given. To a poor one most.

The Ghost turns toward a modest house. A warm window glows through the snow.

INT. CRATCHIT'S DINING ROOM - DAY

BOB CRATCHIT, slight and tired in a brushed threadbare suit and long white comforter, enters carrying TINY TIM. Tim is frail, sandy-haired, braced at the legs, and holds a little crutch. MRS. CRATCHIT, in a twice-turned gown brightened with ribbons, lays the table.

(SOUND: potatoes bubble; children race across the floor; a goose crackles in the oven)

MRS. CRATCHIT
What has ever got your precious father then? And your brother, Tiny Tim?

Bob sets Tim beside the fire. The family brings in a small goose with ceremony.

BOB
And how did little Tim behave?

BOB
As good as gold, and better. He told me, coming home, that he hoped the people saw him in the church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to them to remember upon Christmas Day, who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

The goose is carved. The family eats. Bob raises a humble glass.

(SOUND: knives tap plates; the fire roars; laughter gives way to a brief hush)

BOB
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
Oh, no, kind Spirit! Say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge bows his head. Mrs. Cratchit raises her cup.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I wish I had him here. I'd give him a piece of my mind to feast upon.

BOB
My dear, Christmas Day.

The family resumes its laughter, but Scrooge cannot look away from Tiny Tim.

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - NIGHT

FRED, ruddy-faced and handsome in a warm brown coat, presides over a crowded Christmas party. His wife and friends laugh around a laden table.

(MUSIC: harp and voices rise into a cheerful Christmas song)

FRED
He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live! He believed it too!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There is nothing in the world so irresistibly contagious as laughter and good-humour.

FRED
I am sorry for him; I couldn't be angry with him if I tried. He may rail at Christmas till he dies, but he can't help thinking better of it.

The party roars. Invisible among them, Scrooge laughs and tries to join the games.

FRED
A Merry Christmas and a Happy New Year to the old man, whatever he is!

(SOUND: the toast is answered by a dozen raised glasses)

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The Ghost of Christmas Present has grown visibly older, its dark curls streaked with grey. Scrooge stands beside it as two ragged children cling to the green robe: a gaunt boy and girl, yellow-faced and wolfish.

(SOUND: three quarters ring from a distant clock; the Spirit's torch sputters)

SCROOGE
Are spirits' lives so short?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
My life upon this globe is very brief. It ends to-night.

SCROOGE
What are these?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
They are Man's. This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, and most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom.

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The clock strikes Twelve. The children vanish. A tall, hooded figure, GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME, emerges from the darkness, showing only one pale hand.

(SOUND: twelve strokes roll through the house; the final one leaves absolute silence)

EXT. LONDON EXCHANGE - DAY

The hooded Ghost leads SCROOGE through a crowded exchange. MERCHANT HARRIS, a fat man with a great chin, and MERCHANT WILKINS, red-faced and richly dressed, pause among other businessmen.

(SOUND: coins chink; carriage wheels grind over frozen mud)

MERCHANT HARRIS
I only know he's dead.

MERCHANT WILKINS
When did he die?

MERCHANT HARRIS
Last night, I believe.

MERCHANT WILKINS
What has he done with his money?

MERCHANT HARRIS
I haven't heard. He hasn't left it to me. That's all I know.

Their laughter swallows the dead man's name. The Ghost points onward.

INT. OLD JOE'S SHOP - NIGHT

A low, filthy shop bristles with rusty keys, chains, rags, bones, and stolen goods. OLD JOE, a grey-haired dealer, smokes beside a charcoal stove. MRS. RIGGS, the charwoman, MRS. DILBER, a laundress, and a man in faded black unload bundles.

(SOUND: the shop hinges shriek; coins clatter on a wooden counter)

MRS. RIGGS
He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead!

OLD JOE
Bed-curtains!

MRS. RIGGS
If he wanted to keep 'em after he was dead, a wicked old screw, why wasn't he natural in his lifetime?

MRS. DILBER
It's a judgment on him.

Old Joe counts out their gains. Scrooge sees a bundle of bed-curtains and a shirt stripped from an unseen corpse.

OLD JOE
This is the end of it, you see! He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead!

SCROOGE
The case of this unhappy man might be my own. My life tends that way, now.

INT. BARE BEDROOM - NIGHT

A pale shaft of light falls upon a bare, uncurtained bed. Beneath a ragged sheet lies an unvisited corpse. A cat claws at the door. Rats gnaw beneath the hearth.

(SOUND: scratching at wood; small teeth work beneath the stone)

The Ghost points to the covered head. Scrooge cannot lift the sheet.

SCROOGE
If there is any person in the town who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me, Spirit, I beseech you!

INT. CRATCHIT'S DINING ROOM - DAY

The same poor room is silent. TINY TIM'S little stool stands empty beside the fire. His crutch rests against the wall. BOB CRATCHIT, still slight and threadbare, enters slowly. MRS. CRATCHIT and the children sew around him.

(SOUND: a needle pulls through cloth; the clock ticks in the quiet room)

MRS. CRATCHIT
The colour hurts my eyes.

BOB
I promised him that I would walk there on a Sunday. My little, little child!

He leaves the room, then returns with tears hidden behind a brave smile. The children gather against him.

BOB
However and whenever we part from one another, I am sure we shall none of us forget poor Tiny Tim.

The family holds one another.

BOB
I am very happy.

(SOUND: a chair creaks upstairs; the fire gives a single soft pop)

EXT. CHURCHYARD - NIGHT

A neglected churchyard lies between high walls, overgrown with grass and weeds. The hooded Ghost points to one grave.

(SOUND: winter wind moves through dead grass; a bell tolls far away)

SCROOGE, trembling, approaches the stone. Moonlight reveals the name:

EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only?

The Ghost does not move.

SCROOGE
Men's courses will foreshadow certain ends, to which, if persevered in, they must lead. But if the courses be departed from, the ends will change. Say it is thus with what you show me!

The finger points from the grave to Scrooge and back again.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed?

He falls to his knees.

SCROOGE
No, Spirit! Oh no, no! I am not the man I was. I will not be the man I must have been but for this intercourse.

The Ghost's hand trembles.

SCROOGE
I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

The hooded figure collapses inward, shrinking into a bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - MORNING

The bedpost is Scrooge's own. SCROOGE, still thin and white-haired but now bright-eyed, sits upright in his nightshirt, his face wet with tears.

(SOUND: church bells burst into a glorious peal; clear morning traffic rises below)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Time before him was his own, to make amends in!

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future! The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. A merry Christmas to everybody!

He rushes to the window and throws it open. TOM, a Sunday-clad street boy, looks up from below.

SCROOGE
What's to-day, my fine fellow?

TOM (O.S.)
Why, Christmas Day!

SCROOGE
Do you know the Poulterer's, in the next street but one? The big prize Turkey?

TOM (O.S.)
The one as big as me?

SCROOGE
Go and buy it. Tell 'em to bring it here. Come back in less than five minutes and I'll give you half-a-crown!

Tom runs.

SCROOGE
I'll send it to Bob Cratchit's! It's twice the size of Tiny Tim.

EXT. LONDON STREET - DAY

SCROOGE, dressed in his best coat, walks beneath a clear sky, smiling at everyone. A giant turkey sways in the back of a cab bound for Camden Town.

(SOUND: bells, boots, and Christmas greetings fill the bright street)

The same portly, warm-faced CHARITY GENTLEMAN from the day before approaches. Scrooge takes both his hands.

SCROOGE
My dear sir, how do you do? A merry Christmas to you, sir!

CHARITY GENTLEMAN
Mr. Scrooge?

SCROOGE
Allow me to ask your pardon. Will you have the goodness—

Scrooge whispers in his ear. The gentleman's eyes widen.

CHARITY GENTLEMAN
Lord bless me! My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it, I assure you. Come and see me.

CHARITY GENTLEMAN
I will!

SCROOGE
Thank'ee. Bless you!

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - NIGHT

FRED, still ruddy-faced and handsome, opens the door to find Scrooge, hat in hand and smiling nervously.

(MUSIC: laughter and a fiddle stop in startled silence)

SCROOGE
Fred! It is I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in?

Fred embraces him. The room erupts. Scrooge is drawn into the party, laughing until tears fill his eyes.

(SOUND: chairs scrape back; the party cheers and the music resumes)

INT. SCROOGE'S COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

The next morning, SCROOGE, white-haired and bright-eyed in his black coat, sits behind the open office door. BOB CRATCHIT, slight, threadbare, and breathless, hurries into his little cell eighteen minutes late.

(SOUND: the clock strikes nine; Bob's hurried pen scratches to life)

SCROOGE
What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time.

SCROOGE
You are? I think you are. Step this way, sir, if you please.

Bob edges forward, frightened of the old ruler.

SCROOGE
And therefore I am about to raise your salary!

BOB
Sir?

SCROOGE
A merrier Christmas, Bob, my good fellow, than I have given you for many a year! I'll raise your salary, and endeavour to assist your struggling family. Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

Bob stares, then breaks into stunned laughter.

INT. CRATCHIT'S DINING ROOM - DAY

The same humble room is warm and bright. TINY TIM, alive and sturdier, crosses the floor on his little crutch. SCROOGE stands beside him like a second father while BOB and MRS. CRATCHIT gather the family around the fire.

(SOUND: the family laughs; a kettle sings; chestnuts crack)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Scrooge was better than his word. To Tiny Tim, who did NOT die, he was a second father. He became as good a friend, as good a master, and as good a man as the good old city knew.

Scrooge squeezes Tiny Tim's shoulder. Tim looks toward the fire and raises his cup.

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had no further intercourse with Spirits, but lived upon the Total Abstinence Principle ever afterwards. And it was always said of him, that he knew how to keep Christmas well, if any man alive possessed the knowledge.

(MUSIC: warm voices rise over the fire and bells)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
May that be truly said of us, and all of us! And so, as Tiny Tim observed—

FADE OUT.

THE END.
